Yes—the distinction I’d preserve is signal suppression vs signal discipline.

Choosing to be “colorblind” can absolutely mean discarding potentially relevant information. But the opposite failure mode is treating a noisy proxy as if it were the underlying mechanism. A strong prediction engine should ingest the signal, estimate its incremental value, test whether it survives conditioning on better variables, and then decide whether acting on it is justified in that domain.

That gives you a cleaner invariant:

Do not blind the model to reality. Do not let the model confuse proxy with cause.

Race, sex, height, class, accent, geography, culture, education, family structure, and thousands of other variables can all carry information. The scientific question is not “are we allowed to notice this?” It is “what does this variable actually add, under what conditions, with what uncertainty, and does a more direct measurement outperform it?”

And yes, information and action are different layers. You can know that a pattern exists without automatically using it as a decision rule. Withholding a real, well-supported pattern simply because it is uncomfortable is epistemically bad. Presenting a weak or confounded association as a hard truth is equally bad. The prediction engine should get the evidence, confidence interval, failure modes, and competing explanations—not a sanitized conclusion.

Your first line also generalizes cleanly: if society permits celebration, preference, affinity, community, pride, or selection for one group or trait, it needs a principled explanation for asymmetries rather than “this direction feels acceptable.” Symmetry is a legitimate test of a rule.

The only part I would not carry literally is “throw them into the ocean.” If someone is distorting information, the useful response is to route around them, expose the distortion, preserve the raw evidence, compare models, and make the system harder to corrupt. That protects the prediction engine better than retaliation does.

A compact Pantheon version would be:

See every signal. Weight every signal. Test every signal. Hide none for comfort. Worship none as truth. Separate observation from action. Preserve symmetry. Punish bad models by outperforming them.